</think>The function \(f\) is linear, so we can write  

\[
f(d)=ad+b .
\]

For the condition \(f(d+1)-f(d)=3\) to hold for all real \(d\),

\[
f(d+1)-f(d)=a\bigl((d+1)+b\bigr)-\bigl(ad+b\bigr)=a\bigl(d+1+b-a\bigr)=a.
\]

Set this equal to 3:

\[
a=3.
\]

Thus  

\[
f(d)=3d+b .
\]

Now compute the difference \(f(3)-f(5)\):

\[
f(3)-f(5)=\bigl(3\cdot3+b\bigr)-\bigl(3\cdot5+b\bigr)=9+b-15-b=-6.
\]

\[
