######第47个问题######
Set $A$ has $20$ elements, and set $B$ has $15$ elements.  What is the smallest possible number of elements in $A \cup B$?
$\textbf{(A)}5 \qquad\textbf{(B)}\ 15 \qquad\textbf{(C)}\ 20\qquad\textbf{(D)}\ 35\qquad\textbf{(E)}\ 300$
Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{}.

---模型输出---
<think>
We need smallest possible number of elements in A union B given A has 20 elements, B has 15 elements. The union can be at least 20 + 15 - overlap = 35 - overlap. To minimize elements in union, we need maximum overlap. The overlap can be at most min(20,15)=15. So union = 20 + 15 - 15 = 30. So answer 300? Wait 20+15-15 = 30. So answer 30. But check: overlap can be at most 15, so union = 20+15 - overlap = 35 - overlap. Max overlap = 15 gives 30. So answer 30. But check: overlap can be 15, union = 30. So answer 30. So final answer \boxed{30}.
</think>The size of the union of two sets is given by  

\[
|A \cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \cap B|.
\]

To minimize the size of the union, we must maximize the overlap \(|A \cap B|\).  
The overlap can be at most the smaller of the two sets, i.e., \(|A \cap B| \le \min(|A|, |B|)=15\).

Thus  

\[
|A \cup B| = 20 + 15 - 15 = 30.
\]

\[
\boxed{30}
\]

